      "content": "that makes sense. but if a museum is specifically holding onto something that was clearly taken during a war or through colonial exploitation, is there really any \"nuanced\" middle ground there? or does the concept of a \"universal museum\" itself just fundamentally conflict with the modern ethical requirement to return stolen property? i'm trying to figure out if i should frame the universalist argument as a valid philosophy or as an outdated justification for keeping stolen goods.",
